from pathlib import Path
from bs4 import BeautifulSoup
root=Path('.book-work/galacticvisionsexploringasimovsfoundationseries-v1-956c94de2580')
title='Galactic Visions: Exploring Asimov’s Foundation Series'
sections=[
(None,'''Isaac Asimov’s Foundation series imagines a Galactic Empire facing decline and a plan to shorten the dark age that follows. This book follows that premise through psychohistory, political maneuvering, the preservation of knowledge and the uneasy relationship between individual choice and predicted history. Hari Seldon’s plan promises a path through collapse, but its apparent precision does not settle who should be trusted with directing civilization. The chapters ask readers to weigh the value of foresight against the freedom of people living inside the prediction.''',None),
('Can history be predicted?','''Psychohistory is Asimov’s fictional attempt to model the behavior of large populations mathematically. The first chapter asks whether such prediction would be a science or an art, then considers statistical patterns, complex systems and the limits imposed by unpredictable individuals. Seldon’s calculations can describe broad tendencies, yet an exceptional person may disrupt the expected course. The idea matters because a forecast can change the decisions of the people who hear it. A model of society is never separate from the society that uses it.''','chapter-1'),
('The Seldon Plan and free will','''The second chapter follows historical determinism: the claim that large social forces push events along recognizable paths. The Plan aims to guide the galaxy across a period of disorder, using institutions and knowledge to keep the projected future possible. Yet the narrative repeatedly tests the gap between collective patterns and personal action. Time vaults and the hidden work of the Second Foundation show that prediction also requires intervention. The question becomes whether steering history protects humanity or quietly limits its freedom.''','chapter-2'),
('Why empires decline','''The third chapter traces the Galactic Empire’s weakening and the Foundation’s emergence. Lost technical capacity, corruption and fading political legitimacy make collapse more than a single military defeat. Seldon’s answer is to preserve knowledge within a new institution that can shorten the period of barbarism. The Foundation later uses religion, trade and economic dependence as its power changes. The book compares this arc with theories of historical cycles while asking how much of the future is shaped by institutions rather than inevitable repetition.''','chapter-3'),
('Politics on a galactic scale','''The fourth chapter turns to manipulation and government. Salvor Hardin relies on strategy as much as force, while the Mule reveals how one charismatic figure can upset a vast plan. The Second Foundation’s secret interventions deepen the ethical problem: even if leaders believe they serve a greater good, those they guide may never consent. Through Seldon, Hardin and Bayta Darell, the book examines the tension between long-range planning and the judgment of individuals caught in immediate crises.''','chapter-4'),
('Knowledge against collapse','''The fifth chapter compares the Galactic Empire’s decline with the vulnerabilities of historical empires. Large territories, administrative costs, weakened education and elite corruption erode its capacity to respond. Against this, the Foundation works as a storehouse and transmitter of scientific and cultural knowledge. The chapter also treats knowledge as something living: preservation is useful only when people can adapt and build upon what they inherit. Libraries, learning and critical thought matter as much as any device or archive.''','chapter-5'),
('Events that test the plan','''The final chapter surveys the original Foundation books through Seldon’s warning, Hardin’s early crises, conflict with neighboring kingdoms, the Mule’s conquest and the search for the Second Foundation. These episodes make the larger themes concrete. A plan designed around populations meets rulers, traders, rebels and people whose choices cannot be reduced to an equation. Plot turns do more than provide spectacle; they challenge the promise of control that psychohistory seems to offer.''','chapter-6'),
('Why read the whole book','''The conclusion asks what a civilization can pass to its successors when political order proves fragile. Asimov’s galaxy offers neither simple faith in mathematics nor a rejection of collective planning. It invites readers to consider the obligations attached to knowledge, power and intervention. Start with the psychohistory chapters if the idea of predicted history interests you; continue through the political and knowledge chapters to see how the plan changes when people must live with it.''','conclusion'),
]
out=BeautifulSoup(f'<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>{title} — Ten-minute edition</title></head><body><article data-reader-content></article></body></html>','html.parser')
a=out.article;h=out.new_tag('h1');h.string=title;a.append(h)
for heading,body,anchor in sections:
 if heading:
  h=out.new_tag('h2');h.string=heading;a.append(h)
 p=out.new_tag('p');p.string=body
 if anchor:
  link=out.new_tag('a',href=f'full_content.html#{anchor}');link.string='Read this section in full';p.append(' ');p.append(link);p.append('.')
 a.append(p)
path=root/'book/en/short_content.html';path.write_text(str(out)+'\n')
print('Prepared short',len(a.get_text(' ',strip=True).split()),'words')
